        "description": "<div id=\"remove_no_follow\">\n\t\t<div class=\"grid grid--cols-10@md grid--cols-8@lg article-column \">\n\t\t\t\t\t  <div class=\"col-12 col-10@md col-6@lg col-start-3@lg\">\n\t\t\t\t\t\t<div class=\"article-column__content\">\n<section class=\"wp-block-bigbite-multi-title\"><div class=\"container\"></div></section>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Open the test configuration of almost any front-end project and you\u2019ll find one line that decides more about your CI bill than most of the code around it: <code>environment: 'jsdom'</code>.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">Unit and component tests for React, Vue, Angular, Svelte, and Solid apps rarely run in a real browser. They run in Node.js, on top of a JavaScript implementation of the browser\u2019s Document Object Model. Your test renders a button, clicks it, and asserts on the text that appears. The document, the <code>click()</code>, and the text all come from a library that pretends to be a browser.</p>\n\n\n\n<p class=\"wp-block-paragraph\">For most teams that libra",
